# Who owns what (KAV-109, ADR-0071).
#
# GitHub reads this file two ways: it asks the owner to review a pull request that touches their
# paths, and the branch rule "Require review from Code Owners" can make that review mandatory.
#
# THAT RULE IS OFF HERE ON PURPOSE. This is a one-person repository, and GitHub does not let an
# author approve their own pull request, so a mandatory owner review would lock the only person
# out of merging. What stops a bad change today is the eleven required CI checks on `main`
# (including the promotion guard), not this file. When a second maintainer joins, turn the rule
# on: this file is already the list it needs.
#
# The paths below are the ones where a mistake costs the most. The last matching line wins.

*                       @roshanoommenreji

# The automation that holds credentials and can change production
/.github/               @roshanoommenreji

# What AWS is told to build, and how the cluster is told to run
/infra/                 @roshanoommenreji
/deploy/                @roshanoommenreji

# What the agent is allowed to do, and the code that does it
/policy/                @roshanoommenreji
/services/executor/     @roshanoommenreji

# The release path: what reaches production, and the record of what did
/scripts/release/       @roshanoommenreji
/deploy/promotion/      @roshanoommenreji
